en":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Flipping a Fair Coin","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"eWjp7ltTPk"}],"key":"cEpsUheAGK"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/m08-coin-baf4faa245eca06c562f51fb51edce30.svg","alt":"The two faces of a coin, heads and tails.","key":"EttgYpIWzu","urlSource":"images/gen/m08-coin.svg"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"bVf6OyjWnD"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A coin has two sides; we usually call them “heads” and “tails.” For a “fair” coin (one that is not unevenly weighted, and does not have identical images on both sides) the chances that a “flip” will result in either side facing up are equally likely. Thus, P(heads) = P(tails) = 1/2 or 0.5. Letting ","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"nYHUxbjugX"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"H","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"l7UNfgh9U6"}],"key":"hSDaCbkfoj"},{"type":"text","value":" represent “heads,” we can abbreviate the probability: ","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"XH6ulYQlEx"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"P(H) = 0.5.","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"wZAXwbqk0E"}],"key":"UgfdJ0Gg3x"}],"key":"TPII4VUEST"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Classical probabilities can also be used for more realistic and useful situations. A practical use of a coin flip would be for you and your roommate to decide randomly who will go pick up the pizza you ordered for dinner. A common expression is “Let’s flip for it.” This is because a coin can be used to make a random choice with two options. Many sporting events begin with a coin flip to determine which side of the field or court each team will play on, or which team will have control of the ball first.","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"ievTorIN30"}],"key":"CMr4PbuvjH"}],"class":"tip","key":"VSxFO7gjNr"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Rolling Fair Dice","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"wAQtSO5OpV"}],"key":"bNrqq9A8ck"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/m08-dice-ad34163d700e5867831cf572601ec599.svg","alt":"Two dice, one showing five and one showing three.","key":"VAQn7NwU55","urlSource":"images/gen/m08-dice.svg"}],"enumerator":"2","key":"UoOyXJePgu"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each traditional (cube-shaped) die has six sides, marked in dots with the numbers 1 through 6. On a “fair” die, these numbers are equally likely to end up face-up when the die is rolled. Thus, ","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"ynKtE4Biba"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"P(1) = P(2) = P(3) = P(4) = P(5) = P(6) = 1/6","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(1) = P(2) = P(3) = P(4) = P(5) = P(6) = 1/6</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">P</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">P</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">P</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">P</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">P</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">P</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1/6</span></span></span></span>","key":"sGGmbgbcZm"},{"type":"text","value":" or about 0.167.","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"EGgwqg1Yf5"}],"key":"Q6GMVCVg4k"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here, again, is a practical use of classical probability. Suppose six people go out to dinner. You want to randomly decide who will pick up the check and pay for everyone. Again, the P(each person) = 1/6.","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"key":"dh3CBLPSdy"}],"key":"Ek4g8jDY3J"}],"class":"tip","key":"Lv6ACVSe2M"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Spinners","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"uFAmg2Eko1"}],"key":"chXQzBhflh"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/m08-spinner-6024341e3352ad88ca8e94918167f2f3.svg","alt":"A spinner in which blue takes up half the circle, and red and yellow each take up a quarter.","key":"vNZEABeBSU","urlSource":"images/gen/m08-spinner.svg"}],"enumerator":"3","key":"ldNEmOwYv9"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This particular spinner has three colors, but each color is not equally likely to be the result of a spin, since the portions are not the same size.","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"key":"kihM9yD3uZ"}],"key":"firHU4iFuh"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Since the blue is half of the spinner, P(blue) = 1/2. The red and yellow make up the other half of the spinner and are the same size. Thus, P(red) = P(yellow) = 1/4.","position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"key":"vZQHt6snL8"}],"key":"uHgkxyA6Uq"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Suppose there are 2 freshmen, 1 sophomore, and 1 junior in a study group. You want to select one person. The P(F) = ","position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"key":"IZeBN561fR"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"2/4 = 1/2","position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/4 = 1/2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2/4</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1/2</span></span></span></span>","key":"iyIg1PQZUR"},{"type":"text","value":"; P(S) = 1/4; and P(J) = 1/4, just like the spinner.","position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"key":"bKBiJLvNWn"}],"key":"AE8ciZeVvz"}],"class":"tip","key":"UC9Db8WoYN"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Selecting Students","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"AiJudlAxhj"}],"key":"oG7gxZPBsN"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Suppose we had three students—say, two women and one man—and wished to select one of them randomly. To do this you might have each person write his/her name on a (same-sized) piece of paper, then put the three papers in a hat, and select one paper from the hat without looking.","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"key":"NbuTO0aWwX"}],"key":"zKhhvVf2hv"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Since we are selecting randomly, each is equally likely to be chosen. Thus, each has a probability of 1/3 of being chosen.","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"ZFFcBxUbDY"}],"key":"ptAMUVWA5F"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A slightly more complicated, but more interesting, probability question would be to propose selecting 2 of the students, and ask, “What is the probability that the two students selected will be different genders?”","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"key":"jVlbRjoa7F"}],"key":"rYkRXQKbba"}],"class":"tip","key":"NtHvICiff1"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: Equally Likely Outcomes","position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"key":"bV6k7vmI7s"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-equally-likely-outcomes","label":"Check Your Understanding: Equally Likely Outcomes","html_id":"check-your-understanding-equally-likely-outcomes","implicit":true,"key":"p2W2Z1vQVv"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-ab552a073177c801fc08fc2e7e8860b3.mjs","model":{"question":"A spinner is one-third green, one-third orange, and one-third purple. What is P(orange)?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"1/3","feedback":"Correct! Each of the three equal sections has probability 1/3."},{"correct":false,"text":"1/2","feedback":"1/2 would be right only if orange took up half the spinner."},{"correct":false,"text":"2/3","feedback":"The three probabilities must sum to 1, and each section is the same size."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The sections are equal in size.","explanation":""},"key":"knMt6o4T3y"}],"key":"w3W2XIPCEa"}],"key":"z8WhpL1UYC"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"What Is Probability? Language and Notation","url":"/pages/m08-introduction-to-probability-3-of-5","group":"Section 5: Introduction (Probability)"},"next":{"title":"The Law of Large Numbers: Probability in the Long Run","url":"/pages/m08-introduction-to-probability-5-of-5","group":"Section 5: Introduction (Probability)"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}